msgid "Simplified Hibernate with Quarkus Data Hibernate"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus Data Hibernate is the perfect solution if you want to get started "
"using Hibernate in Quarkus."
msgstr ""

msgid "This technology is considered {extension-status}."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In _experimental_ mode, early feedback is requested to mature the idea.\n"
"There is no guarantee of stability nor long term presence in the platform "
"until the solution matures.\n"
"Feedback is welcome on our https://groups.google.com/d/forum/quarkus-"
"dev[mailing list] or as issues in our https://github.com/quarkusio/quarkus/"
"issues[GitHub issue tracker]."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For a full list of possible statuses, check our https://quarkus.io/faq/#what-"
"are-the-extension-statuses[FAQ entry]."
msgstr ""

msgid "Walk-through"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Let's take a progressive approach to learning how to use Quarkus Data "
"Hibernate, and start with a simple entity."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This guide is not going to go into the specifics of using either\n"
"xref:hibernate-orm.adoc[Hibernate], or its underlying\n"
"link:{jakarta-persistence-spec-url}[Jakarta Persistence specification],\n"
"because both already have great documentation that you can and should use "
"for more in-depth knowledge."
msgstr ""

msgid "Importing the extension, and configuration"
msgstr ""

msgid "You also have to configure the Hibernate processor, which is required:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Add the relevant configuration properties in `{config-file}`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"xref:datasource.adoc[Configure the datasource] for production, relying on "
"xref:datasource#dev-services[Dev Services] for connection information in "
"tests / dev mode."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Configure Hibernate ORM to create the schema on startup in production, which "
"is useful for experimentation, but rely on xref:hibernate-orm.adoc#dev-"
"mode[convenient defaults in tests / dev mode]."
msgstr ""

msgid "Our first entity"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"What this guide will focus on is the basics of how to use Quarkus Data "
"Hibernate, so let's start with how to\n"
"create an entity:"
msgstr ""

msgid "In order to map a Java class to a Database table, just:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Write a class"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Annotate it with link:{jakarta-persistence-javadocs-url}/jakarta/persistence/"
"entity[`@Entity`]"
msgstr ""

msgid "Make it extend `ManagedEntity`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Make its fields `public`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"And that's it, now you can start creating instances of your entity, persist "
"it to your database, after which point\n"
"all changes to its fields will be automatically sent to the database (no "
"need for any explicit `update` instruction),\n"
"and even remove it from the database:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This is required on any method which interacts with the database, in order "
"to run the operations in a transaction."
msgstr ""

msgid "Our first repository"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Now that we know how to create, update and delete our entities, let's see "
"how we can query our database to find them,\n"
"or even run delete queries."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Because query operations do not belong on instances of our entities, we will "
"place these operations on another type\n"
"called a `Repository`, and because those queries are intimately tied with "
"the entities they operate on, we recommend\n"
"placing them on an interface nested in the entity:"
msgstr ""

msgid "A Quarkus Data Hibernate repository is:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An interface nested in the entity (though it could be a toplevel interface "
"if you prefer)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Which extends the `ManagedRepository` interface, with the entity in question "
"as type parameter"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"And contains query methods, annotated with either `@Find` or `@HQL` or even "
"`@SQL` (for native queries), or\n"
"`default` methods with implementation of queries"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can use `@Find` methods to find single instances, or collections of "
"entities, by using the parameters of the\n"
"method to build a query. The Hibernate documentation has\n"
"https://docs.jboss.org/hibernate/orm/7.1/introduction/html_single/"
"Hibernate_Introduction.html#generated-finder-methods[all the information you "
"need about this]."
msgstr ""

msgid "Finder methods may return collections of entities"
msgstr ""

msgid "Or generate `COUNT` queries"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Or single entities, and any number of parameters which must all match the "
"queried entities."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Alternately, you can use `@HQL` or `@SQL` queries, which support HQL and SQL "
"queries. Once again\n"
"the Hibernate documentation has https://docs.jboss.org/hibernate/orm/7.1/"
"introduction/html_single/Hibernate_Introduction.html#generated-query-"
"methods[all the information you need]."
msgstr ""

msgid "Query methods may return collections of entities"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Or projections of a single column, even multiple columns with `Object[]` and "
"`List<Object[]>` types"
msgstr ""

msgid "Queries can of course refer to method parameters."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The beauty of generated finder and query methods is that they are type-"
"checked at build time: they will be validated\n"
"and guarantee that you did not make any typo in the entity names, their "
"fields, the HQL/SQL syntax, or the name of\n"
"their parameters."
msgstr ""

msgid "Using the repository"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Using the repository can be done by simply injecting it where you intend to "
"use it:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Now, as an added benefit, you can also access the repository using a handy "
"shortcut generated static method on the\n"
"https://docs.jboss.org/hibernate/orm/7.1/introduction/html_single/"
"Hibernate_Introduction.html#static-metamodel[generated Metamodel]\n"
"of your entity, which is super useful for discovery of operations (just type "
"`Cat_.repo().` and see all your methods)\n"
"and to avoid making a detour outside of your method to add an injected "
"field, such as in this startup code that\n"
"will delete all your cats (don't do this in production!!):"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"All the repositories you define as nested interfaces of your entity will be "
"available under their generated metamodel\n"
"class under accessor methods of the same name as the repository. This is "
"strictly equivalent to injecting the repository\n"
"type, and in fact uses CDI under the hood to look up the repository."
msgstr ""

msgid "The <code>ManagedRepository</code> super type"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Just like in previous versions of Hibernate ORM and Hibernate Reactive with "
"Panache, the `ManagedRepository` type comes\n"
"packed with most of the operations you need to work on your entity, such as, "
"out of the box:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Non-type-safe queries"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"With generated finder and query methods, as we've previously shown, "
"everything is validated at build-time, but if\n"
"you want to write non-type-safe queries, you can always use the provided "
"methods of `ManagedRepository`:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Sorting is applied on the `PanacheQuery` returned by `find()` and "
"`findAll()`, not on the shortcut\n"
"`list()`, `listAll()`, `stream()`, or `streamAll()` methods. For a single "
"criterion, pass a `Sort`\n"
"directly — there is no need to wrap it in `Order.by()`. Use `Order` only "
"when you need multiple\n"
"criteria, or when receiving an `Order` from a REST endpoint:"
msgstr ""

msgid "About entity identifiers"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In the example above, we extended the `ManagedEntity` type, and did not "
"define any database identifier, that's\n"
"because `ManagedEntity` comes with a default generated database identifier, "
"so you don't have to worry about it. It\n"
"does this by extending the `WithId.AutoLong` class, which provides a "
"generated database identifier of type `Long`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can choose to extend the `WithId.AutoString` for a `String` identifier, "
"or `WithId.AutoUUID` for a `UUID`\n"
"identifier to automatically get an attribute of the form:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Naturally, you can also provide your own database identifier explicitly and "
"implement the `ManagedEntity.CustomId`\n"
"interface in your entity, as well as use the `ManagedRepository.CustomId` "
"interface for your repository, in order to\n"
"specify you database identifier type:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Here is a list of entity supertypes you can use and the ID type they "
"provide, but keep in mind you do not have to\n"
"extend any of these types if you define you own ID:"
msgstr ""

