msgid "Use stateless sessions"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Out of the box, your subtype of `ManagedEntity` will be managed by Hibernate "
"ORM, and every change to the entity will\n"
"be automatically sent to the database without requiring any explicit "
"`update` operation."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If on the other hand, you wish to make every `update` operation explicit, "
"then you need to use what Hibernate ORM calls\n"
"a _stateless session_."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In this case, you need to extend the `RecordEntity` class and extend the\n"
"`RecordRepository` interface:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"As you can see, your entity definition is exactly the same, besides the two "
"interfaces. But now, your entities will\n"
"not be _managed_, so every update operation has to be explicit:"
msgstr ""

msgid "As you can see, the only differences with a managed entity are:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Any change to the entity instance fields must be explicitly pushed to the "
"database by calling `update()` either on\n"
"the entity or its repository"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You need to call `insert()` instead of `persist()` to insert the entity in "
"the database"
msgstr ""

msgid "Your session will be of type `StatelessSession` instead of `Session`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"But that's mostly it. Everything else stays the same, in particular for "
"queries and how to obtain entities."
msgstr ""

msgid "Let&#8217;s get reactive"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You want to start using your entities in a reactive application? Let's start "
"by importing Hibernate Reactive in your\n"
"`pom.xml` as well as a data source for your database:"
